f solution. If you have 0.5 moles of a substance dissolved in 2 liters of solution, you can calculate the molarity by multiplying the number of moles by the volume in liters:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Molarity} = \\frac{\\text{moles of solute}}{\\text{liters of solution}} = \\frac{0.5 \\text{ moles}}{2 \\text{ liters}} = 0.25 \\text{ M}\n   \\]\n2. **Reaction Stoichiometry**: In chemical reactions, the stoichiometry (the quantitative relationship between reactants and products) is often determined using multiplication. For example, in the balanced chemical equation:\n   \\[\n   2 \\text{H}_2 + \\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow 2 \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\n   \\]\n   If you start with 4 moles of hydrogen gas (H\u2082) and 2 moles of oxygen gas (O\u2082), you can use multiplication to determine the limiting reactant and the amount of water (H\u2082O) produced:\n   - Hydrogen gas: \\(4 \\text{ moles H}_2 \\times \\frac{1 \\text{ mole H}_2\\text{O}}{2 \\text{ moles H}_2} = 2 \\text{ moles H}_2\\text{O}\\)\n   - Oxygen gas: \\(2 \\text{ moles O}_2 \\times \\frac{2 \\text{ moles H}_2\\text{O}}{1 \\text{ mole O}_2} = 4 \\text{ moles H}_2\\text{O}\\)\n   Since hydrogen gas is the limiting reactant, you can produce 2 moles of water.\n\n3. **Radiation Exposure**: In radiation science, the absorbed dose (in units of gray) is calculated by multiplying the absorbed energy by the mass of the substance. For example, if a substance absorbs 5 joules of energy and has a mass of 0.1 kilograms, the absorbed dose is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Absorbed dose} = \\frac{\\text{absorbed energy}}{\\text{mass}} = \\frac{5 \\text{ joules}}{0.1 \\text{ kg}} = 50 \\text{ gray}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Population Growth Models**: In population biology, the growth rate of a population can be modeled using exponential growth equations. For example, if a population grows at a rate of 2% per year, the population after \\(t\\) years can be calculated using the formula:\n   \\[\n   P(t) = P_0 \\times (1 + r)^t\n   \\]\n   where \\(P_0\\) is the initial population, \\(r\\) is the growth rate, and \\(t\\) is the time in years. Multiplication is used to calculate the population at different time points.\n\n5. **Astronomy**: In astronomy, the distance between celestial bodies is often calculated using multiplication. For example, the distance between Earth and the Sun is approximately 149.6 million kilometers, which can be calculated by multiplying the average distance by the number of planets:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance to the Sun} = 149.6 \\times 10^6 \\text{ km} \\times 8 \\text{ planets} = 1.1968 \\times 10^{10} \\text{ km}\n   \\]\n\nThese examples illustrate how multiplication is fundamental in various scientific contexts, enabling precise calculations and understanding of natural phenomena.\n</think>\n<answer>. Multiplication is crucial in various scientific contexts, especially in calculations involving measurements, rates, and proportions. Here are a few examples:\n1. **Molarity Calculations**: In chemistry, molarity (the number of moles of solute per liter of solution) is calculated by multiplying the number of moles by the volume in liters. For instance, if you have 0.5 moles of a substance dissolved in 2 liters of solution, the molarity is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Molarity} = \\frac{0.5 \\text{ moles}}{2 \\text{ liters}} = 0.25 \\text{ M}\n   \\]\n2. **Reaction Stoichiometry**: In chemistry, the stoichiometry of chemical reactions is determined using multiplication. For example, in the balanced chemical equation:\n   \\[\n   2 \\text{H}_2 + \\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow 2 \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\n   \\]\n   If you start with 4 moles of hydrogen gas (H\u2082) and 2 moles of oxygen gas (O\u2082), you can use multiplication to determine the limiting reactant and the amount of water (H\u2082O) produced:\n   - Hydrogen gas: \\(4 \\text{ moles H}_2 \\times \\frac{1 \\text{ mole H}_2\\text{O}}{2 \\text{ moles H}_2} = 2 \\text{ moles H}_2\\text{O}\\)\n   - Oxygen gas: \\(2 \\text{ moles O}_2 \\times \\frac{2 \\t